          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas vs Electric vs Reel vs Robot: Which Type Fits Your Yard?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beyond individual products, the type of mower you choose matters more for a small yard than it does for a large property, since the trade-offs of each type show up faster on a lawn you're mowing every week.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery electric mowers</strong> are the right default choice for most small yards in 2026. Modern batteries like EGO's 56V platform genuinely match gas-mower cutting power on lawns under half an acre, while eliminating fumes, pull-cord starting, and most engine maintenance. The main downside is upfront cost and a finite runtime that requires planning around a charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Corded electric mowers</strong> are the best value option when your yard is small enough and close enough to an outlet that cord management isn't a real burden. You get unlimited runtime at a fraction of a battery mower's price, at the cost of literally being tethered while you mow.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Reel mowers</strong> make sense only for small, flat, well-maintained lawns mowed on a strict weekly schedule. They're the cheapest, quietest, and most maintenance-free option, but they punish any lapse in mowing frequency and can't handle slopes or rough terrain.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Robot mowers</strong> are the premium, convenience-first choice for small yards with a clearly defined shape and no narrow passages. The upfront cost and boundary-wire setup are real commitments, but the payoff — never mowing again — is unmatched by any other type.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gas mowers</strong> remain relevant for buyers who prioritize unlimited runtime and long-term mechanical reliability over noise and emissions, but for a genuinely small yard, the case for gas has weakened considerably as battery technology has closed the performance gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            One factor that doesn't get enough attention in this comparison is total cost of ownership over five years, not just the sticker price on day one. A gas mower's upfront cost is often lower than a comparable battery mower, but factor in gas, oil, air filters, spark plugs, and the occasional carburetor cleaning, and the gap narrows considerably. Battery mowers have a higher entry cost concentrated in the battery itself, but from that point on, ongoing costs are close to zero beyond electricity. Corded electric and reel mowers remain the cheapest to own over any time horizon, since neither has a battery to eventually replace nor an engine to maintain.
          </p>
        </div>

