          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Mowing Tips That Make a Small Lawn Look Its Best
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Follow the One-Third Rule</h3>
          <p>
            Never remove more than one-third of the grass blade's height in a single mow. Cutting shorter than that stresses the lawn, exposes soil to weeds, and often requires more frequent watering to recover. On a small lawn, this usually means mowing weekly during peak growing season rather than letting grass get away from you and scalping it back down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vary Your Mowing Pattern</h3>
          <p>
            Mowing the same direction every time compacts soil and trains grass to lean, creating a streaked, uneven look. Alternate between vertical, horizontal, and diagonal passes each time you mow — an easy habit on a small yard where the extra planning takes seconds. On a lawn this size, a simple mental rotation between three patterns is enough to keep the grass growing upright and evenly over a full season, rather than developing the permanent lean that comes from always mowing the same well-worn path.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Blades Sharp</h3>
          <p>
            A dull blade tears grass rather than cutting it cleanly, leaving ragged, browning tips that make even a freshly mowed lawn look unhealthy within a day or two. Sharpen rotary mower blades at least once per season, and check a reel mower's cutting edge more frequently since dull reels struggle noticeably with thicker grass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mow When the Grass Is Dry</h3>
          <p>
            Wet grass clumps, clogs the deck, and tears rather than cuts cleanly, whether you're using a battery mower or a reel mower. Wait until morning dew has evaporated or avoid mowing shortly after rain for the cleanest results.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Storage & Maintenance -->
        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Storage and Maintenance for Small-Yard Mowers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small yards often come with small storage spaces, which is part of why deck width and folding design matter as much as cutting power when choosing a mower. Most battery and corded electric mowers on this list feature handles that fold flat and decks that let the mower stand upright, cutting floor footprint by more than half compared to a fixed-handle gas mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance needs vary sharply by type. Battery mowers require the least ongoing care — occasionally cleaning grass buildup from the underside of the deck and storing the battery at a moderate charge level over winter. Corded electric mowers need essentially the same light deck cleaning with no battery to manage at all. Reel mowers need periodic blade sharpening, which can be done with an at-home sharpening kit. Gas mowers require the most upkeep: seasonal oil changes, air filter checks, spark plug replacement, and fuel stabilizer if the mower sits unused over winter.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Winterizing Tip</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              For gas mowers, either run the tank dry or add a fuel stabilizer before storing for winter — leftover gas can gum up the carburetor over several months of inactivity. For battery mowers, store the battery separately from the mower at roughly 40-60% charge rather than fully charged or fully depleted, which extends long-term battery lifespan.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

